00:00:10.215 --> 00:00:11.111
Problem.

6
00:00:12.361 --> 00:00:20.297
For a search box, return the 3 most popular past queries that start with what the user has typed, on every keystroke, fast.

7
00:00:20.577 --> 00:00:24.737
Popularity changes as users search, and trending queries should rise.

8
00:00:26.087 --> 00:00:26.961
Part 2.

9
00:00:27.241 --> 00:00:28.159
Key idea.

10
00:00:29.409 --> 00:00:31.414
A trie maps each prefix to a node.

11
00:00:31.694 --> 00:00:39.481
A DFS of the whole subtree on every keystroke is too slow for short prefixes like j, so cache the top-K at every node.

12
00:00:39.761 --> 00:00:41.787
A read is then O(prefix length).

13
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:46.099
A write updates the top-K along its path (O(L, K log K)).

14
00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:49.878
Reads outnumber writes enormously, so this trade-off is right.

15
