96
00:06:49.096 --> 00:06:51.187
What changes from the interview version.

97
00:06:51.467 --> 00:06:52.811
Here is a quick table:

98
00:06:53.091 --> 00:07:15.981
Interview, and Production (Spring Boot 4). trie nodes with a top-K list: one Redis sorted set per prefix ( at most 10 characters), holding at most K terms. update on every keystroke: update from the search log (submitted queries), ideally batched. no normalisation: lower-case, trim, collapse spaces; filter for prefixes longer than 10 characters. no caching:

99
00:07:16.261 --> 00:07:19.291
Cache-Control: public, max-age=60.

100
00:07:19.571 --> 00:07:22.387
Most prefixes are shared by millions of users.

101
00:07:23.737 --> 00:07:26.851
Memory is bounded: prefixes times K entries.

102
00:07:27.131 --> 00:07:35.963
Add per-user personalisation as a second, small set merged at read time, and remove offensive terms through a deny-list filter on write.

103
00:07:37.313 --> 00:07:38.188
Part 8.

104
00:07:38.468 --> 00:07:39.577
Common mistakes.

105
00:07:40.827 --> 00:07:46.139
A DFS on every keystroke for one-letter prefixes, which can mean millions of nodes.

106
00:07:46.419 --> 00:07:49.961
Updating the trie synchronously on every search at Google scale.

107
00:07:50.241 --> 00:07:58.305
Aggregate counts offline (hourly or daily) and rebuild or swap the tries, with a small real-time layer for trending queries.

108
00:07:58.585 --> 00:08:03.939
Ignoring normalisation (case, whitespace, accents) and offensive-content filtering.

109
00:08:05.289 --> 00:08:06.185
Part 9.

110
00:08:06.465 --> 00:08:07.809
Follow-up questions.

111
00:08:09.059 --> 00:08:09.934
Question.

112
00:08:10.214 --> 00:08:12.945
How would you serve this to 100k QPS?

113
00:08:13.225 --> 00:08:14.590
Thirty-second answer:

114
00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:19.841
Shard the trie by the first one or two characters, replicate it, and keep it in memory.

115
00:08:20.121 --> 00:08:24.067
Put a CDN or edge cache in front for very common prefixes.

116
00:08:24.347 --> 00:08:30.129
The client debounces keystrokes (about 100 ms) and caches responses per prefix.

117
00:08:31.479 --> 00:08:32.353
Question.

118
00:08:32.633 --> 00:08:34.617
How do you personalise suggestions?

119
00:08:34.897 --> 00:08:36.263
Thirty-second answer:

120
00:08:36.543 --> 00:08:44.137
Blend global top-K candidates with the user's own history and context (location, language) in a re-ranking step.

121
00:08:44.417 --> 00:08:49.025
The trie supplies candidates; a small model or scoring function orders them.

122
